PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.2/10 (bar 8), 0 level(s) below 6/10: A solid, fair and well-ordered set (all trap rates 0, unique by line logic, clean teach→practise→twist→boss→breather→finale rhythm). Its main weaknesses: the intent text is often more clever than the board (several levels describe a single mechanical chain), the "aha" is repeated (anchor row/column, then chain) rather than varied, a few pictures have wasted empty space or cluttered stamps, and some of the early teaching levels are too similar. The strongest levels are the ones where a mechanic produces a real deduction (voids cutting runs, stamps pinning a slide, cracked clues bounded by columns). The weakest are the generic-silhouette fillers and the late-game boards that rely on sheer size for difficulty. Curve: Strong, well-structured 10-chapter ramp. Each chapter introduces one mechanic alone (fill/X, voids, stamps, colour-cross, smudged, cracked, chain, combos), then combines it, with a breather at slot 7 and a boss/finale at slot 6 and 12. The visible gaps are these. (1) Chapter 1 teaches little beyond the basics: L1-L2 are near-identical "exact-fit then X" tutorials, and "choices at start" is already 14-15. (2) The mechanic order is odd. Colour (ch.4) arrives before voids and stamps, which are simpler. Colour-cross is taught in ch.5 via a 3-colour board with no stepping stone. (3) The late game is flat. Many levels share the same "one exact row/column anchors, then chain" insight, and the intent text for L96-L120 repeats it. Chapters 8-10 read as variations rather than new ideas. (4) The difficulty spikes are mostly on target. Late casual rates of 55-65% are on spec, but L81 (61%), L82 (59%) and L89 (57%) sit in a long run of ~55-60% levels with no relief. Chapter 9's breather (L115, 90%) is good, but L113 and L114 sit right before it at 55-56%. (5) Ch.8 smudged and ch.7 cracked levels are mostly "one line is full, then read the hidden line from crossings", which is mechanical once learned. Sharper twists are needed (e.g. a smudged line that cannot be read from crossings alone and needs a second-order deduction). (6) The easy-run rule is fine. Overall the set is polished and fair, with zero trap rates, but it is more competent than memorable.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L2 (6/10): Intent mixes exact fit, X and column 3 3 in a single level; a sailboat of 8x7 reads similarly to L1 (X-from-1s then fill). Too many ideas for slot 2. → Make the whole level one exact-fit chain (row 4 -> cols) and drop the column 5 3 3 branch, or swap with a simpler overlap-free board.
L3 (6/10): Jellyfish is small and the picture is hard to recognise; overlap taught alongside X marks. Casual 93% is fine. → Redraw with clearer tentacles (7x7) so overlap is the single new idea.
L7 (6/10): Breather is easy (92%) but is almost the same as L1/L2 (full rim row, exact columns); sand bucket is plain. → Swap for a 10x8 picture with a distinct silhouette (e.g. lantern) keeping three passes.
L9 (6/10): Repeats L2/L7 style 'exact-fit column then read'; Anchor is fine but not new. → Make it rely on a split clue in a column so exact-fit is combined with X's rather than repeated.
L13 (6/10): 'Reach' is described as new but the board is two exact-fit columns (1 8 and 1 7); it is not actually reach-teaching. → Use a board where a column's run must touch a known filled cell (stem with only one placement), e.g. with a single full floor row and 3 stems.
L16 (6/10): Hedgehog quills in rows 1-2 are isolated single cells = busywork; legs at bottom mostly empty. → Thicken the spines and add a face to make the end fewer lonely dots.
L21 (6/10): Bear silhouette is mostly a blob and the legs are repetitive; chain repeats L15/L17. → Add a distinctive feature (salmon, hump) and vary the leg clues.
L25 (6/10): Colour lesson is clear but the strawberry is bland; two-colour start has little colour-specific logic. → Keep the rule, add a row where colours touch to force the lesson earlier.
L33 (6/10): Awning bottom half is sparse single-cell posts; empty rows 4-7. → Add a goods display in the empty rows.
L39 (6/10): Bust is ambiguous and 11x13 with face details is fiddly. → Give the face stronger features.
L41 (6/10): Wreath is highly sparse in interior (void heart) so the board is mostly void; hard to read. → Shrink the void or add a ribbon.
L51 (6/10): Seahorse rely on stamp with 8 passes; slow serial chain. → Add a second entry point.
L56 (6/10): Toucan reads poorly, sparse right half; casual 74% right after a 90% breather. → Redraw the toucan larger within the width.
L59 (6/10): Swan hard to recognise. → Redraw neck curve.
L64 (6/10): Watering can unrecognisable, 74% and a spike versus neighbours. → Redraw.
L69 (6/10): 34 moves and 7 passes, rows crowded. → Shrink.
L81 (6/10): Entirely hidden column and a 61% rate after an easy breather; the logic is long. → Give one anchor in the smudged column.
L82 (6/10): Lantern boat 59% casual hovering; smudged row edges ambiguous. → Stamp one edge cell.
L83 (6/10): 60% and pinwheel is hard to read. → Clarify hub.
L86 (6/10): Mug is nearly all full rows; 2 passes is too trivial after L85. → Add a harder crack.
L89 (6/10): 57% for a twist; reading column constraints is tedious. → Reduce.
L90 (6/10): Two cracks in one 55%. → Remove one.
L93 (6/10): Kiln has lots of dotted-noise columns. → Simplify.
L95 (6/10): Noodle bowl sparse. → Add chopsticks.
L99 (6/10): 63% and hat is a triangle. → Add details.

level-design score 7.2/10 — A solid, fair and well-ordered set (all trap rates 0, unique by line logic, clean teach→practise→twist→boss→breather→finale rhythm). Its main weaknesses: the intent text is often more clever than the board (several levels describe a single mechanical chain), the "aha" is repeated (anchor row/column, then chain) rather than varied, a few pictures have wasted empty space or cluttered stamps, and some of the early teaching levels are too similar. The strongest levels are the ones where a mechanic produces a real deduction (voids cutting runs, stamps pinning a slide, cracked clues bounded by columns). The weakest are the generic-silhouette fillers and the late-game boards that rely on sheer size for difficulty.
